\section PydanticSpecOverview Overview

\subsection PydanticSpecOverviewBackground Background: what the Pydantic models replaced

Developers familiar with the previous Dakota input-spec workflow should
know three things up front:

<ol>
<li><b>dakota.xml / NIDR is no longer the starting point for input-spec
    changes.</b>  The Pydantic models in
    \c dakota/python/dakota/spec are now the canonical specification of
    Dakota's input grammar.  They serve the same purpose as \c dakota.xml
    did: defining the hierarchy of Dakota keywords, one-of relationships,
    expected argument types, constraints on arguments, and mappings from
    keywords into Dakota's internal representation.  Grammar edits and
    additions begin here.</li>

<li><b>The Data classes have been replaced by an intermediate
    representation (IR).</b>  User input is no longer stored as plain
    member variables (ints, strings, \c vector<double>, etc.) on Data
    class instances set by keyword handlers.  Instead it is stored in maps
    keyed by string IR keys (ProblemDescDB keys).  The IR
    plays the same role as the Data classes and is consumed by
    \c ProblemDescDB in the same way.</li>

<li><b>DAKOTA_GENERATE_JSON_SCHEMA is the new ENABLE_SPEC_MAINT.</b>
    Configure Dakota with this CMake option turned on to regenerate
    build artifacts from the Pydantic models.</li>
</ol>

\subsection PydanticSpecOverviewModels The Pydantic models

A Pydantic model is a Python class whose fields carry type annotations
and optional metadata.  Pydantic uses these annotations to <em>validate</em>
a JSON document or nested Python dict: it verifies that the document is
structurally correct and fills in any missing optional fields with their
declared defaults.  The result is a validated model instance with a
complete, type-checked representation of the user's input.

Developers new to Pydantic should read the
<a href="https://docs.pydantic.dev/latest/concepts/models/">Pydantic models
concepts page</a> before proceeding; the broader
<a href="https://docs.pydantic.dev/latest/">Pydantic documentation</a>
is the authoritative reference.

The models for Dakota's input grammar live in \c dakota/python/dakota/spec/.
They are organized by keyword block:
<ul>
<li>\c environment.py &mdash; environment block keywords</li>
<li>\c method/ &mdash; a sub-package with one module per method
    (e.g. \c method/sampling.py, \c method/polynomial_chaos.py)</li>
<li>\c model.py &mdash; model block keywords</li>
<li>\c variables.py &mdash; variables block keywords</li>
<li>\c interface.py &mdash; interface block keywords</li>
<li>\c responses.py &mdash; responses block keywords</li>
<li>\c shared/ &mdash; mixins and sub-models reused across blocks. XML entities were translated to classes in shared/. Inclusion of a shared model usually occurs through inheritance.</li>
<li>\c base.py &mdash; \c DakotaBaseModel and supporting infrastructure</li>
</ul>

\subsection PydanticSpecOverviewFlow Information flow

<b>At build time</b> CMake drives two code-generation passes from the
Pydantic models:

<ol>
<li>When the CMake variable \c DAKOTA_GENERATE_JSON_SCHEMA is on, the
    models are used to export a JSON schema, which is copied to
    \c dakota/src/dakota.json if it changed.  The JSON schema is a
    portable, language-neutral description of Dakota's input grammar,
    similar in role to \c dakota.input.nspec.  \c src/dakota.json is
    committed, so builds with the option off use the existing copy;
    commit the regenerated file along with your model changes.</li>

<li>CMake generates C++ source files for the IR from the JSON schema.
    These generated files are analogous to \c NIDRProblemDescDB: they
    contain keyword handlers that populate the IR from validated user
    input.  Two hand-maintained registry files are also inputs to this
    step:
    <ul>
    <li>\c src/default_overrides_registry.json &mdash; IR key defaults that
        cannot be derived from the Pydantic models (see
        \ref PydanticSpecIrDefaults).</li>
    <li>\c src/default_policy_registry.json &mdash; type-based fallback
        defaults (zero, false, empty) for IR keys not covered by the
        other sources.</li>
    </ul>
    CMake also generates C++ source files for the freeform input file
    parser from the JSON schema.</li>
</ol>

<b>At run time</b> the input parser reads a \c .in file (or accepts JSON
directly) and produces a validated JSON document.  The validation step
verifies structure, inserts defaults, and computes derived fields such as
inferred variable bounds.  IR handlers then walk the validated JSON and
populate the IR, which \c ProblemDescDB exposes to the rest of Dakota.

\subsection PydanticSpecOverviewWhatToEdit What to edit when updating the input spec

For most grammar changes you will edit only the Pydantic models.  You
will also need to edit \c src/default_overrides_registry.json if you
add an IR key whose correct runtime default cannot be inferred from the
model &mdash; for example, a key that the C++ side initializes to a non-zero
enum value.  \c src/default_policy_registry.json is for type-wide
fallback policies and rarely needs touching for individual keyword
additions.  See \ref PydanticSpecIrDefaults for the full rules.

After editing the models, regenerate the JSON schema and the IR source
files (configure with \c DAKOTA_GENERATE_JSON_SCHEMA enabled, or run the
codegen scripts directly; see step 7 of \ref PydanticSpecProcedure).

\subsection PydanticSpecOverviewAbstractions Key abstractions

The Pydantic models in \c dakota/python/dakota/spec constitute the canonical
specification of Dakota's input grammar. Implement grammar edits and
additions in these Python models and, where required, provide matching
C++ validation or computed-field implementations so that the shared
grammar contract remains consistent between schema generation and runtime.

Most models inherit from \c DakotaBaseModel
(\c dakota/python/dakota/spec/base.py). Parsed fields are declared with
\c DakotaField and represent user-visible keywords and arguments.
\c ValidationRule subclasses capture checks or mutations that depend on
multiple parsed fields. \c ComputedFieldSpec entries paired with
\c @computed_field properties export derived values in the schema
or validated models. This page is self-contained: procedural steps reference
the detailed authoring sections that follow; readers may consult any
section on demand.

\section PydanticSpecProcedure Procedure: Adding a New Keyword

\subsection PydanticSpecProcedureOrdinary Ordinary keyword

Most new keywords follow this pattern: declare the field, describe how it
maps to the IR, regenerate, and test.  If the keyword holds a one-of
selection among named sub-options, also follow the additional steps in
\ref PydanticSpecProcedureUnion.

<ol>
<li><b>Find the right model class.</b>
Locate the \c DakotaBaseModel subclass for the keyword's block and context
(e.g. a sampling method keyword goes in
\c dakota/python/dakota/spec/method/sampling.py).
See \ref PydanticSpecOverviewModels for the package layout.</li>

<li><b>Declare the field.</b>
Add a typed class attribute and declare it with \c DakotaField, working
through three decisions.

<b>Optionality.</b>  Choose based on what happens when the user omits the
keyword:
<ul>
<li>Required — no \c default and no \c | None.  Pydantic enforces
    presence.</li>
<li>Optional with a grammar default — supply \c default=value or
    \c default_factory=....  Omission inserts that default into the
    validated model.</li>
<li>Optional, absent means disabled — add \c | None to the annotation and
    \c default=None.  Omission yields \c None.</li>
</ul>

<b>Type.</b>  Choose the annotation based on what the keyword accepts:
\htmlonly
<table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="4">
<thead>
<tr><th>Keyword form</th><th>Type annotation</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
  <td>Flag (keyword alone, no argument)</td>
  <td><code>Literal[True] | None</code></td>
  <td>Typically uses presence-based materialization; common choices are
      <code>PRESENCE_TRUE</code>, <code>PRESENCE_FALSE</code>, and
      <code>PRESENCE_LITERAL</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Integer argument</td>
  <td><code>int</code> or <code>int | None</code></td>
  <td>Typically uses <code>DIRECT_VALUE</code> materialization to store
      the argument in the IR.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Real argument</td>
  <td><code>DakotaFloat</code> or <code>DakotaFloat | None</code></td>
  <td>Typically uses <code>DIRECT_VALUE</code> materialization.  Use
      <code>DakotaFloat</code> (not plain <code>float</code>) for any
      field that may hold <code>NaN</code>, <code>inf</code>, or
      <code>-inf</code>.  See \endhtmlonly \ref PydanticSpecFloat \htmlonly.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>String argument</td>
  <td><code>str</code> or <code>str | None</code></td>
  <td>Typically uses <code>DIRECT_VALUE</code> materialization to store
      the argument in the IR.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Multiple values</td>
  <td><code>list[int]</code>, <code>list[str]</code>, etc.</td>
  <td>Typically uses <code>DIRECT_VALUE</code> materialization to store
      the argument list in the IR.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>Sub-keywords (nested block)</td>
  <td>A <code>DakotaBaseModel</code> subclass</td>
  <td>A keyword cannot have both a literal argument and sub-keywords.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td>One-of selection (mutually exclusive sub-options)</td>
  <td><code>Union[BranchA, BranchB]</code></td>
  <td>See \endhtmlonly \ref PydanticSpecProcedureUnion \htmlonly for the
      additional steps.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
\endhtmlonly

<b>Annotation details.</b>  Supply \c description.  Add \c aliases for
backward-compatible spellings.  Add Pydantic constraint kwargs (\c gt,
\c ge, \c lt, \c le, \c min_length, etc.) where applicable.
See \ref PydanticSpecDakotaField.</li>

<li><b>Add materialization metadata.</b>
In the \c dakota dict of the \c DakotaField, add a \c materialization list
that describes how the field's value is written to the IR.  Each entry needs
at minimum \c ir_key, \c storage_type, and \c ir_value_type.  Pick the
\c storage_type that matches the IR write shape (e.g. \c DIRECT_VALUE for a
scalar written straight through, \c PRESENCE_LITERAL when presence selects
an enum value).  Look at an existing field with similar behavior as a
template.  See \ref PydanticSpecMaterialization.</li>

<li><b>Handle the IR default.</b>
If you gave the field a grammar-level default in step 2, the codegen will
extract it automatically — nothing more to do.  If the C++ side initializes
the IR key to a non-zero value that must be preserved when the user omits
the keyword, add an explicit entry to
\c dakota/src/default_overrides_registry.json.
See \ref PydanticSpecIrDefaults.</li>

<li><b>Regenerate and verify.</b>
Build with \c DAKOTA_GENERATE_JSON_SCHEMA enabled (or run
\c extract_defaults_from_schema.py then \c generate_ir_tables.py directly).
Confirm the new \c ir_key, its \c ir_value_type, and its default appear
correctly in the generated output and that no collision errors are
reported.</li>

<li><b>Test.</b>
Add or update Python unit tests for parsing and schema output.  Run
integration tests that exercise the keyword through \c ProblemDescDB reads.
Update example inputs and documentation as needed.</li>
</ol>

\subsection PydanticSpecProcedureUnion Keyword anchoring a Union

A keyword anchors a Union when its value is a one-of selection among
mutually exclusive named sub-options.  Follow the ordinary keyword steps
above, with these additions:

<ol>
<li><b>Choose a union pattern.</b>
The pattern determines whether omission is allowed, whether there is a
default branch, and what an empty \c {} means.  See
\ref PydanticSpecUnions for the six patterns and their semantics.</li>

<li><b>Declare the field as a Union type.</b>
Annotate the field with \c Union[BranchA, BranchB] (and \c | None
if omission is permitted).  Set \c union_pattern in the \c dakota dict and,
if the pattern specifies a default branch, set \c model_default to its name.
Keep the Python annotation, \c default/\c default_factory, \c union_pattern,
and \c model_default consistent with each other.</li>

<li><b>Place materialization correctly.</b>
Each branch model carries \c DakotaField materialization entries for the
values it contributes.  The Union field itself may also carry
materialization when its own presence or absence should write to the IR —
for example, a \c PRESENCE_TRUE entry to set an enabled flag regardless of
which branch the user selects.  If the Union field has no such effect on
the IR, it carries no materialization.</li>
</ol>

\subsection PydanticSpecProcedureSpecial Less common cases

<ul>
<li><b>Keyword that names another block (pointer).</b>
Use \c block_pointer in the \c DakotaField \c dakota dict and keep the
field a plain \c str with no nested structure.  See
\ref PydanticSpecPointers.</li>

<li><b>New method or model family.</b>
Create a module in \c method/ or \c model/, define a config class and a
single-field selection wrapper, and import the wrapper in the package
\c __init__.py.  See \ref PydanticSpecAddModel.</li>

<li><b>Correctness depends on multiple fields.</b>
Author a \c ValidationRule subclass, attach it via \c _VALIDATION_RULES,
and provide matching C++ implementation and registration.
See \ref PydanticSpecValidation.</li>

<li><b>Derived value needed in the validated model or schema.</b>
Add a \c ComputedFieldSpec to \c _COMPUTED_FIELDS, implement the
\c @computed_field property, and register the corresponding C++ function.
See \ref PydanticSpecValidation.</li>
</ul>

\section PydanticSpecUnions How Union fields are processed

\subsection PydanticSpecUnionWhy Why different patterns are necessary

Pydantic's built-in Union handling is structural: it tries each branch type
in order and accepts the first that validates.  That mechanism handles type
discrimination but loses two things Dakota requires.

<b>Explicit omission semantics.</b>  Dakota's grammar distinguishes between
a keyword that is absent (feature disabled, or a grammar-level default kicks
in) and one that is present without arguments (feature enabled with C++
defaults).  Structural discrimination gives no hook for this distinction.
The six patterns encode the full set of combinations through the Python type
annotation and \c default / \c default_factory choices, so that code
generators can emit correct IR defaults for every reachable state.

<b>Named discriminators for code generation.</b>  Every Union branch in
Dakota is a <em>single-field wrapper model</em> whose one field name is the
user-visible keyword.  For example, the \c trend Union field has branches
\c TrendNone (field name: \c none), \c ExperimentalGPTrendLinear (field
name: \c linear), and so on.  The model class name serves as a stable handle
in \c model_default, in schema annotations, and in generated IR write tables.
Structural discrimination would require guessing at field shapes, which is
fragile as models evolve.

Accepted user input for a Union field is therefore a single-key dictionary
whose key matches one branch's field name, for example \c {"linear": true}
or \c {"retry": 3}.  \c DakotaBaseModel adds a model-level validator that
rejects input containing more than one branch key.

The \c dict branch that appears in Patterns 3, 5, and 6 is a deliberate
catch-all sentinel matching an empty dictionary \c {}, which represents
"feature enabled with no explicit sub-selection."  Without it, there would
be no way to express "turn this on but supply no arguments" without inventing
a spurious flag field.

Always record \c union_pattern in the \c DakotaField \c dakota dict.  This
documents the intended semantics for other developers and allows code
generators to emit the correct IR defaults for omitted branches.

\subsection PydanticSpecUnionPattern1 Pattern 1: Optional with model default

Python shape: \c Union[OptionA, OptionB] with a \c default_factory.

Semantics: omission yields a meaningful default branch selected at grammar
time.

Use this when there is a sensible built-in choice that most users will not
need to override.

Example — GP surrogate trend function (\c model.py \c ExperimentalGPConfig):

\code
# Each branch wraps exactly one user-visible keyword.
class TrendNone(DakotaBaseModel):
    none: Literal[True] = DakotaField(default=True, ...)

class ExperimentalGPTrendLinear(DakotaBaseModel):
    linear: Literal[True] = DakotaField(default=True, ...)

class ExperimentalGPTrendReducedQuadratic(DakotaBaseModel):
    reduced_quadratic: Literal[True] = DakotaField(default=True, ...)

# Union field uses default_factory to name the default branch.
trend: Union[
    TrendNone,
    ExperimentalGPTrendLinear,
    ExperimentalGPTrendReducedQuadratic,
] = DakotaField(
    default_factory=ExperimentalGPTrendReducedQuadratic,
    description="Polynomial trend function",
    dakota={"union_pattern": 1, "model_default": "ExperimentalGPTrendReducedQuadratic"},
)
\endcode

<ul>
<li>Omitting \c trend silently selects \c reduced_quadratic.</li>
<li>Writing <tt>trend reduced_quadratic</tt> selects the same branch explicitly.</li>
<li>Writing <tt>trend linear</tt> selects \c ExperimentalGPTrendLinear.</li>
<li>Writing <tt>trend none</tt> disables the trend function.</li>
</ul>

The \c model_default string must equal the class name of the
\c default_factory exactly; the code generator uses it to record the
grammar-level default in the JSON schema.

\subsection PydanticSpecUnionPattern2 Pattern 2: Optional with no default branch

Python shape: \c Union[OptionA, OptionB] | None with \c default=None.

Semantics: omission disables the feature; the user must write a branch
keyword to enable it.

Use this for optional features that are off by default with no meaningful
auto-selection.

Example — MARS surrogate interpolation type (\c model.py \c MarsConfig):

\code
# Branch wrapper models; the field name on each is the user-visible keyword.
class InterpLinear(DakotaBaseModel):
    linear: Literal[True] = DakotaField(default=True, ...)

class InterpCubic(DakotaBaseModel):
    cubic: Literal[True] = DakotaField(default=True, ...)

interpolation: Union[InterpLinear, InterpCubic] | None = DakotaField(
    default=None,
    description="MARS model interpolation type",
    dakota={"union_pattern": 2},
)
\endcode

<ul>
<li>Omitting \c interpolation leaves it disabled (\c None).</li>
<li>Writing <tt>interpolation linear</tt> selects \c InterpLinear.</li>
<li>Writing <tt>interpolation cubic</tt> selects \c InterpCubic.</li>
</ul>

\subsection PydanticSpecUnionPattern3 Pattern 3: Three-state optional with default branch

Python shape: \c Union[OptionA, OptionB, dict] | None with a \c default_factory.

Semantics: omission => disabled (\c None); \c {} => enabled with the
grammar-level default branch (from \c default_factory); named branch =>
enabled with explicit configuration.

Use this when a feature has three semantically distinct states and there is a
grammar-level default configuration worth recording in the schema.  The
\c DakotaBaseModel validator converts omission to \c None before validation
while converting \c {} to a \c default_factory instance, preserving all three
states.

This pattern is currently unused in the Dakota spec; it is documented here for
features that may need it in future grammar additions.

Hypothetical example:

\code
my_feature: Union[FeatureA, FeatureB, dict] | None = DakotaField(
    default_factory=FeatureA,
    description="Optional feature with grammar-level default",
    dakota={"union_pattern": 3, "model_default": "FeatureA"},
)
\endcode

<ul>
<li>Omitting \c my_feature => \c None (disabled).</li>
<li>Writing <tt>my_feature {}</tt> => enabled, \c FeatureA defaults applied.</li>
<li>Writing <tt>my_feature { feature_b ... }</tt> => enabled, \c FeatureB selected.</li>
</ul>

\subsection PydanticSpecUnionPattern4 Pattern 4: Required Union

Python shape: \c Union[OptionA, OptionB] with no \c default and no \c | None.

Semantics: user must select exactly one branch; omission is a validation
error.

Use this for top-level type-selection fields where every valid study
requires an explicit choice.

Example — response type (\c responses.py \c ResponsesConfig):

\code
class ObjectiveFunctions(DakotaBaseModel):
    objective_functions: int = DakotaField(description="Number of objective functions", ...)

class CalibrationTerms(DakotaBaseModel):
    calibration_terms: int = DakotaField(description="Number of calibration terms", ...)

class ResponseFunctions(DakotaBaseModel):
    response_functions: int = DakotaField(description="Number of response functions", ...)

response_type: Union[ObjectiveFunctions, CalibrationTerms, ResponseFunctions] = DakotaField(
    description="Response Type",
    dakota={"anchor": True, "union_pattern": 4},
)
\endcode

<ul>
<li>Omitting \c response_type raises a validation error.</li>
<li>Writing <tt>objective_functions 2</tt> selects \c ObjectiveFunctions.</li>
<li>Writing <tt>calibration_terms 3</tt> selects \c CalibrationTerms.</li>
<li>Writing <tt>response_functions 4</tt> selects \c ResponseFunctions.</li>
</ul>

\subsection PydanticSpecUnionPattern5 Pattern 5: Three-state optional without grammar default

Python shape: \c Union[OptionA, OptionB, dict] | None with \c default=None.

Semantics: omission => disabled (\c None); \c {} => enabled with C++
implementation defaults (no grammar-level default recorded in the schema);
named branch => enabled with explicit configuration.

Use this when a feature has an on/off state independent of its configuration
and no grammar-level default makes sense — enabling it with an empty block
defers entirely to the C++ side for initialization.

Example — surrogate cross-validation (\c model.py \c Metrics):

\code
class Folds(DakotaBaseModel):
    folds: int = DakotaField(description="Number of cross-validation folds", ...)

class Percent(DakotaBaseModel):
    percent: DakotaFloat = DakotaField(description="Percent of data per fold", ...)

cross_validation: Union[Folds, Percent, dict] | None = DakotaField(
    default=None,
    description="Perform k-fold cross validation",
    dakota={"union_pattern": 5, ...},
)
\endcode

<ul>
<li>Omitting \c cross_validation disables it (\c None).</li>
<li>Writing <tt>cross_validation</tt> (bare keyword, no arguments) enables it with C++ defaults.</li>
<li>Writing <tt>cross_validation folds = 10</tt> enables it with 10 folds.</li>
<li>Writing <tt>cross_validation percent = 0.1</tt> enables it with 10% per fold.</li>
</ul>

The difference from Pattern 3: Pattern 5 has \c default=None (not a
\c default_factory), so there is no grammar-level default branch and \c {}
falls through to C++ initialization rather than invoking a factory.

\subsection PydanticSpecUnionPattern6 Pattern 6: Required parent with optional subselection

Python shape: \c Union[OptionA, OptionB, dict] with no \c | None.

Semantics: the parent field must be present; \c {} is a valid "no
subselection" choice; named branches add explicit sub-configuration.

Use this when the enclosing feature is always required but internal
sub-configuration is optional — the user must write the parent keyword but
need not choose a sub-variant.

This pattern is currently unused in the Dakota spec; it is documented here for
future features that may need it.

Hypothetical example:

\code
sub_config: Union[OptionA, OptionB, dict] = DakotaField(
    description="Required parent; optional sub-selection",
    dakota={"union_pattern": 6},
)
\endcode

<ul>
<li>Omitting \c sub_config raises a validation error (no \c | None).</li>
<li>Writing <tt>sub_config {}</tt> enables it with no sub-selection.</li>
<li>Writing <tt>sub_config { option_a ... }</tt> enables \c OptionA.</li>
</ul>

\subsection PydanticSpecUnionRuntime Runtime validation rules

<ul>
<li>If a \c Union has multiple branches, exactly one branch must be
    selected.</li>
<li>Validated model instances matching a branch are accepted.</li>
<li>Dictionaries with more than one key fail validation.</li>
<li>Unrecognized keys fail validation.</li>
<li>If \c dict is a branch, empty-dictionary semantics depend on the
    pattern.</li>
<li>For Pattern 3, omission converts to \c None while \c {} instantiates
    from \c default_factory, preserving three states.</li>
</ul>

\subsection PydanticSpecUnionAuthoring Authoring guidance

<ul>
<li>Record the intended pattern with \c union_pattern.</li>
<li>Set \c model_default when the pattern specifies a default branch;
    its value must equal the \c default_factory class name exactly.</li>
<li>Keep the Python annotation, \c default/\c default_factory,
    \c union_pattern, and \c model_default consistent with each other.</li>
<li>Patterns 3 and 6 are currently unused; prefer Patterns 1, 2, 4, or 5
    for new grammar unless neither fits.</li>
</ul>

\section PydanticSpecDakotaField DakotaField authoring

\c DakotaField extends \c pydantic.Field with a \c dakota dictionary that
controls parser, schema, and materialization behavior. Standard Pydantic
kwargs (\c default, \c default_factory, \c description, \c gt, \c ge,
etc.) remain valid; see the
<a href="https://docs.pydantic.dev/latest/concepts/fields/">Pydantic fields
reference</a> for the full set.

Common \c dakota keys:
<ul>
<li>\c aliases: \c list[str] or \c str for accepted alternative
    spellings.</li>
<li>\c block_pointer: string naming the referenced block family/role.</li>
<li>\c argument: child field treated as inline parameter.</li>
<li>\c anchor: boolean \c True to attach branches to parent context.</li>
<li>\c union_pattern: integer pattern id for one-of semantics.</li>
<li>\c model_default: default branch name for patterns with defaults.</li>
<li>\c internal_only: boolean to reject user input for the field.</li>
<li>\c materialization: \c list[dict] describing IR writes and presence
    effects.</li>
</ul>

Notes:
<ul>
<li>Use \c aliases only for backward compatibility.</li>
<li>Use \c block_pointer when the keyword names another block.</li>
<li>Use \c argument when a child field acts as the inline argument.</li>
<li>Use \c anchor=True only when grammar requires attached
    discriminators.</li>
<li>\c internal_only fields are rejected from user input by
    \c DakotaBaseModel.</li>
</ul>

Example:
\code
directory_tag: Literal[True] | None = DakotaField(
    default=None,
    description="Tag evaluation directories with identifiers",
    dakota={"aliases": ["dir_tag"]},
)
\endcode

\section PydanticSpecMaterialization Materialization and IR writes

Materialization maps validated Pydantic JSON into Dakota's IR. The models
describe valid input, \c dakota["materialization"] prescribes how input affects
IR keys, and the IR codegen emits write-instruction tables.

Pipeline:
- Models export JSON schema with \c x-materialization metadata.
- Codegen scripts under \c dakota/src/ir_codegen read schema and produce IR
  contracts and write tables.
- At runtime, validated JSON is traversed and generated write instructions
  populate IR keys consumed by \c ProblemDescDB.

Key scripts:
- \c dakota/src/ir_codegen/extract_defaults_from_schema.py
- \c dakota/src/ir_codegen/generate_ir_tables.py

Materialization item shape:
- \c ir_key: destination IR key.
- \c storage_type: write-handler identifier.
- \c ir_value_type: Dakota IR type (\c Real, \c size_t,
  \c StringArray, \c short, etc.).
- \c stored_value: optional literal for presence-driven writes.
- Additional handler-specific properties as required.

Common materialization cases:
These are the \c storage_type values authors are most likely to use when
adding or editing grammar.

\htmlonly
<table border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="4">
<thead>
<tr>
  <th><code>storage_type</code></th>
  <th>expected fields</th>
  <th>when to use</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
  <td><code>DIRECT_VALUE</code></td>
  <td><code>ir_key</code>, <code>ir_value_type</code></td>
  <td>Write the field's validated value directly to the IR key. Use for
  1:1 scalar or collection values.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>ADD_TO_VALUE</code></td>
  <td><code>ir_key</code>, <code>ir_value_type</code></td>
  <td>Add the field's value into an existing numeric aggregate instead of
  replacing it. Use for roll-up counters.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>PRESENCE_TRUE</code></td>
  <td><code>ir_key</code>, <code>ir_value_type</code></td>
  <td>Set the target IR key to boolean true when the field is present.
  Use for presence-driven boolean flags.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>PRESENCE_FALSE</code></td>
  <td><code>ir_key</code>, <code>ir_value_type</code></td>
  <td>Set the target IR key to boolean false when the field is present.
  Use for presence-driven negating flags.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>PRESENCE_LITERAL</code></td>
  <td><code>ir_key</code>, <code>ir_value_type</code>, <code>stored_value</code>[, <code>enum_scope</code>]</td>
  <td>Write a literal <code>stored_value</code> when the field is present. Use when
  presence selects a specific enumerated or literal runtime value.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>PRESENCE_ENUM</code></td>
  <td><code>ir_key</code>, <code>ir_value_type</code>, <code>stored_value</code>[, <code>enum_scope</code>]</td>
  <td>Presence selects one of a set of enumerated IR values. Use when the
  field is a symbolic enumerant.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>AUGMENT_ENUM</code></td>
  <td><code>ir_key</code>, <code>ir_value_type</code>, <code>stored_value</code></td>
  <td>Augment an enum-like IR setting with an additional symbolic value.
  Use when presence should extend an enumerated configuration.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>TYPE_DATA_COMBINED</code></td>
  <td><code>ir_key</code>, <code>ir_value_type</code>, <code>stored_value</code>, <code>secondary_ir_key</code>, <code>secondary_ir_value_type</code></td>
  <td>Composite handler: primary literal selection plus secondary direct
  value write. Use when a symbolic choice and an argument are required.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
  <td><code>METHOD_PIECEWISE</code></td>
  <td><code>ir_key</code>, <code>ir_value_type</code>, <code>stored_value</code>, <code>secondary_ir_key</code>, <code>secondary_ir_value_type</code>, <code>secondary_literal_value</code></td>
<td>Composite handler for piecewise method semantics; expands into
  primitive writes during codegen. Use for piecewise method configs.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
\endhtmlonly

